<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Nerchinsk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1689">1689</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Nerchinsk, signed on 27 August 1689 at Nerchinsk in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, was the first treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>. Following clashes over the Amur, including the Qing sieges of Albazin, it fixed the frontier along the Argun River and the Stanovoy Range, leaving the Amur basin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and allowed cross-border trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> interpreters negotiated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. It governed Russo-Chinese relations until the 1850s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From about 1640 Russians entered the Amur basin from the north, into land the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> claimed. By the 1680s the Qing had completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> proper, and by 1685 most of the Russians had been driven from the area; the Qing twice overran the outpost at Albazin, in 1685 and 1686.</p><p>The Russian government knew the Amur could not be defended and sent Fyodor Golovin east as plenipotentiary. Fighting between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> delayed the talks, and Golovin moved to Nerchinsk. Both sides had reasons to settle: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was beginning a war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> wished to turn to the Dzungar Mongols and wanted a fixed frontier.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Qing troops under Songgotu arrived in July 1689, and talks went on from 22 August to 6 September. Latin was the language used. The Russian translator was a Pole, Andrei Bielobocki, and the Qing side relied on the Jesuits Jean-François Gerbillon and Thomas Pereira.</p><p>Tents were set up side by side so that neither side would seem to visit the other. The treaty was signed on 27 August 1689 by Songgotu for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> and Golovin for the tsars Peter I and Ivan V. The authoritative text was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, with translations into Russian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a>, and no official Chinese text existed for another two centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The border ran along the Argun River to the Shilka, up the Shilka and the Gorbitsa, along the watershed of the Stanovoy Mountains and down the Uda to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-okhotsk">Sea of Okhotsk</a>. Albazin was to be abandoned and destroyed. Fugitives who had settled before the treaty stayed, those arriving later were returned, and travel and trade across the frontier were allowed with proper documents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> yielded the land north of the Amur up to the Stanovoy Range but kept the region between the Argun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-baikal">Lake Baikal</a> and gained access to Chinese markets for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>.</p><p>The border west of the Argun was not defined. The arrangement lasted until the mid-nineteenth century, when the Treaty of Aigun in 1858 and the Convention of Peking in 1860 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the Amur lands and the Pacific coast.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Nerchinsk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Nerchinsk</b> of 1689, Russia’s first treaty with Qing China, fixed a border north of the Amur, leaving the river to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
